{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "נבוב לוחות תעשה אותו", :translit "nevuch luchot ta'aseh oto", :gloss "hollow of boards you shall make it"} {:he "נבוב", :translit "nevuch", :gloss "hollow"} {:he "ששים", :translit "shishim", :gloss "sixty"} {:he "ששים אמות", :translit "shishim amot", :gloss "sixty cubits"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "נבוב", :b "ששים"}], :frame {:text "The Torah specifies that the altar must be hollow and made of boards (Exodus 27:8). The Mishnah records that the altar may be enlarged, since its required dimensions are stated as a minimum; the Mishnah states that one may add to the altar's dimensions as long as certain proportions are maintained regarding the ramp and the base (Zevachim 62a).", :sources ["Exodus 27:8" "Zevachim 62a"]}} {:quotes [{:he "אורך החצר מאה באמה ורחב חמשים בחמשים", :translit "orech hechatzer me'ah ba'amah ve'rechav chamishim ba'chamishim", :gloss "length of the court one hundred cubits and width fifty by fifty"} {:he "כחלק אחד מחמשים שבהר הבית", :translit "ke'chelek echad michamishim shebe'har habayit", :gloss "as one part of fifty that is in the Temple Mount"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Geometric proportion: Tabernacle court area (100×50 amot) equals one fiftieth of the Temple Mount."}], :frame {:text "The Mishnah states that the Temple Mount measured five hundred cubits by five hundred cubits (Middot 2:1). The Tabernacle court at one hundred by fifty cubits (Exodus 27:9–12) thus constitutes exactly one part in fifty of that area. Ba'al HaTurim references his earlier comment where he established this proportional relationship.", :sources ["Exodus 27:9-12" "Middot 2:1"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :gematria, :a "נבוב", :a-value 60, :b "ששים", :b-value 650, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? false} {:kind :other, :note "Geometric proportion: Tabernacle court area (100×50 amot) equals one fiftieth of the Temple Mount.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :exodus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:19:36.884418040Z", :model "glm-5.2"}